1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Bd3 Nxd4 8. Nxd4 cxd4 9. Ne2 Bc5 10. O-O O-O 11. Qe1 f5 12. Kh1 a5 13. Rg1 b6 14. g4 Ba6 15. Qg3 Bxd3 16. cxd3 g6 17. b3 b5 18. Bb2 Qb6 19. h4 fxg4 20. Qxg4 Rf7 21. Rg2 Nf8 22. Ng3 Kh8 23. Rc1 a4 24. Rgc2 Nd7 25. b4 Bxb4 26. Rc6 Qd8 27. Bxd4 Bd2 28. Rf1 Nf8 29. f5 gxf5 30. Nxf5 Qe8 31. Rg1 Ng6 32. Nd6 Qxc6 33. Qxe6 Qc7 34. Nxf7+ Kg7 35. Qf6+ Kg8 36. e6 Kf8 37. Nd6+ Kg8 38. Qh8# {Source: O A Jackson, "Forever a 180", p.171. Contributed by Steve Giddins, 7 January 2025. Jackson's book gives the game as played on 31/12/89. But the crosstable for 1989-90 shows the two players did not play each other. There is still greater confusion, because Jackson writes that "the following year...I played him again in the same opening". 1-0, 24 moves. But the 1990-91 crosstable shows that Relange did not play. Another Frenchman called Resende played, but did not meet Oliver! JS edit: I think the game was played in round 6 of the 1988/89 tournament, as the crosstable shows this pairing and result, though this still does not explain the reference to meeting again the following year as this did not happen (unless they met in the Hastings Weekend Open?).} 1-0

